findings.evidence:
Cookie() derives the number of cookie parts from the cookie NAME sent by the client and allocates on it without any bound:

  117  cookieUnder := fmt.Sprintf("%s_", cfg.Cfg.Cookie.Name)
  118  if strings.HasPrefix(cookie.Name, cookieUnder) {
  123      xOFy := strings.Replace(cookie.Name, cookieUnder, "", 1)
  124      xyArray := strings.Split(xOFy, "of")
  125      if numParts == -1 {
  126          if numParts, err = strconv.Atoi(xyArray[1]); err != nil { ... }
  130          cookieParts = make([]string, numParts)

The only guard is that Atoi parses; any decimal integer is accepted. A request carrying the header `Cookie: <cookieName>_1of2000000000` reaches line 130 with numParts = 2e9, i.e. make([]string, 2e9) = 32 GB of zeroed string headers for one request, from a cookie name of about 30 bytes. Nothing upstream can bound it: the value is data in the request, not a body size.
